I mean, this is New York, the city of brotherly love.

247
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:51,560
That's Philadelphia.

248
00:12:52,780 --> 00:12:55,960
Oh, well, Luke, in a city like this, the windy city...

249
00:12:55,960 --> 00:13:01,600
Chicago. But my point is, is that you are entitled to a decent place to live.

250
00:13:02,140 --> 00:13:03,680
And I know just how to get you one.

251
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:06,680
Follow me. The City of Hope.

252
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:11,960
Hospital. Oh, no, no, no, no, wait.

253
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:14,280
Tedesco's just gonna turn me over to the Bureau of Child Welfare.

254
00:13:14,380 --> 00:13:15,380
No, no, no, no, no, no.

255
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:16,480
Sid, you can listen.

256
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:19,060
I promise that's the one place we will not call.

257
00:13:19,340 --> 00:13:22,260
You animal regulation people do nothing but pussyfoot!

258
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:27,560
That cat is prowling around this building with his jack -o' -lantern eyes and his

259
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:30,540
sandpaper tongue, and I want him stopped!

260
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:34,760
Mr. Chidesco. I found him.

261
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:35,800
Hallelujah. Where was he?

262
00:13:35,800 --> 00:13:36,220
Out in the hall?

263
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:37,120
No, in the storeroom.

264
00:13:37,580 --> 00:13:39,120
Gnawing on a dead mouse, I presume.

265
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:41,520
No, no, but he was hungry.

266
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:42,680
I mean, I'm sure he doesn't have a home.

267
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:44,260
Well, boo -hoo -hoo.

268
00:13:44,980 --> 00:13:46,340
He can't stay here.

269
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:48,060
Yeah, listen, would you just watch what you say?

270
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:49,100
He's right outside the hall.

271
00:13:49,420 --> 00:13:50,240
Oh, forgive me.

272
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:52,540
I wouldn't want to injure his oh -so -tender feeling.

273
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:56,440
Yeah, I think he is kind of worried about what might happen to him.

274
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:58,280
Well, let me put his little mind to rest.

275
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:02,920
They'll send over a truck for you, run you downtown, and throw you in a cage.

276
00:14:05,580 --> 00:14:07,480
That sounds kind of harsh, don't you think?

277
00:14:07,580 --> 00:14:09,200
I mean, I was hoping maybe he could be adopted.

278
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:13,040
Well, maybe if he was young and cute, but his age.

279
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:15,020
They'll keep him for two weeks.

280
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:16,760
If no one wants him, they'll put him to sleep.

281
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:19,480
That's the sickest thing I've ever heard.

282
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:20,460
This is nothing.

283
00:14:20,580 --> 00:14:23,800
In the old days, they'd club him with a blunt object and cut him up for catastrophe.

284
00:14:24,980 --> 00:14:28,120
We are talking about a 15 -year -old boy here.

285
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:29,340
Stay with me, Siever.

286
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:30,620
We're talking about a cat.

287
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:32,640
We are talking about Luke Brower.

288
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:34,080
He's been living in the storeroom.

289
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:35,060
And the cat is his?

290
00:14:35,080 --> 00:14:36,340
Would you forget about the cat?

291
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:39,980
We have a homeless boy living here.

292
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:42,820
What? This is a school.

293
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:44,240
We can't have kids here.

294
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:47,320
I'll call the Bureau of Child Welfare.

295
00:14:47,580 --> 00:14:48,660
No, no, no, no, no.

296
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:50,460
I promised him we'd do something else.

297
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:52,780
There is nothing else.

298
00:15:01,580 --> 00:15:05,120
Where's Mike? Where the devil is Mike?

299
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:07,660
Where the heck is Mike?

300
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:11,000
I'm home. Where's Mike?

301
00:15:12,180 --> 00:15:15,100
I wonder if Kate knows any teenage babes.

302
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:21,380
Imagine that. Kate, you and Maggie both having the same dress.

303
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:25,960
Really? I hadn't noticed.

304
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:29,520
Well, no, they're probably different sizes, though.

305
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:36,100
I mean... Not that that matters.

306
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:39,940
Um... Where the hell is Mike?

307
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:44,640
Well, I think we've waited long enough.

308
00:15:44,820 --> 00:15:49,920
Kate, we know how much Mike thinks of you, so we are glad for this opportunity to

309
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:52,560
welcome you to a real Seaver family dinner.

310
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:53,640
Oh, thank you, Mrs.

311
00:15:53,860 --> 00:15:55,620
Maggie. S -siever.

312
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,260
May I offer you a slice of Jason's grandmother's famous blue -ribboned dill bread?

313
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:04,080
Does this bread have yeast in it?

314
00:16:04,260 --> 00:16:06,220
Yes. Isn't it fluffy?

315
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:08,400
Oh, I'm sorry.

316
00:16:08,520 --> 00:16:10,480
I'm afraid I don't eat anything with yeast.

317
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:18,420
Well, I do, so I will, and then we'll pass it to...

318
00:16:18,940 --> 00:16:20,440
...Toby. What's the matter?

319
00:16:20,440 --> 00:16:21,720
Are you allergic to yeast?

320
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:22,760
Oh, no, no.

321
00:16:22,760 --> 00:16:25,180
It's just that it can throw your whole system out of whack.

322
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,180
You know, lately I've been thinking of giving up yeast.

323
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:32,320
Beautiful friends who could give me some pointers.

324
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:36,720
Here you go, sweetheart.

325
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:38,360
I don't want to get wacky.

326
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:45,340
Now, Chrissy, your grandmother ate this bread every day of her life for 95 years.

327
00:16:45,560 --> 00:16:46,320
Watch your stuff.

328
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:48,300
Well, she died.

329
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:55,980
Pass. Hey, hey, hey, there he is.

330
00:16:56,040 --> 00:16:57,640
There's Mike. Oh, wonderful.

331
00:16:57,840 --> 00:16:59,040
I'll get the main course.

332
00:16:59,280 --> 00:17:01,580
Mm -hmm. Aww.

333
00:17:02,460 --> 00:17:03,760
Don't look, Carol.

334
00:17:04,840 --> 00:17:06,480
I got driven out of my dorm room.

335
00:17:06,940 --> 00:17:09,240
I don't like it any better than you guys do.

336
00:17:09,260 --> 00:17:09,840
Deal with it.

337
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:11,540
Oh, hi, Kate.

338
00:17:11,640 --> 00:17:13,300
Hi. So, where's Mike?

339
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:14,900
Oh, he's late.

340
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:16,240
Very, very late.

341
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,220
Well, that's all right.

342
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:21,040
We'll have a lovely time without him.

343
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:25,540
I wonder if anybody will notice if I get up and leave right now.

344
00:17:27,320 --> 00:17:28,560
Well, here it is.

345
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:31,220
Roast beef with all the fixins.

346
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:33,500
Carol, what are you doing here?

347
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:34,460
I'm your daughter.

348
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:35,300
We love each other.

349
00:17:35,340 --> 00:17:37,040
Will you people get off my back?

350
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:41,180
Kate, they say that the first slice is lucky.

351
00:17:41,260 --> 00:17:44,420
Um, didn't Mike mention my diet to you?

352
00:17:44,820 --> 00:17:51,280
Diet? Now, why would an attractive young woman like yourself need to lose weight,

353
00:17:51,540 --> 00:17:52,060
Kate? Come on.

354
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:52,760
I say go ahead.

355
00:17:52,760 --> 00:17:53,700
Eat up. Oh, no, no.

356
00:17:53,700 --> 00:17:55,100
It's not a weight loss diet.

357
00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:57,020
It's more of, um, a way of life.

358
00:17:57,260 --> 00:17:58,520
You're a vegetarian?

359
00:17:58,900 --> 00:17:59,820
Oh, no, no.

360
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:03,140
Not exactly. I just don't eat anything with a face.

361
00:18:07,160 --> 00:18:11,100
I'm sure the roast beef is wonderful, but I just can't bear the idea that it used to

362
00:18:11,100 --> 00:18:11,720
be a cow.

363
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:17,120
A cow? Now, here you go, sweetheart.

364
00:18:17,320 --> 00:18:19,880
You have always loved roast beef.

365
00:18:20,140 --> 00:18:25,520
A cow? Okay, Shirley, you can have some of the vegetables.

366
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:27,240
Oh, do you mind if I wash them off?

367
00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:29,200
I washed them off before I cooked them.

368
00:18:30,420 --> 00:18:32,460
Yes, but they were cooked in the meat juice.

369
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:35,080
I wasn't aware meat juice had a face.

370
00:18:37,900 --> 00:18:42,260
Um, is there anything here at all on the table that you can have?

371
00:18:42,260 --> 00:18:44,700
Is this tap water?

372
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:49,780
Yes. Oh, then, no.

373
00:18:56,320 --> 00:19:00,300
So, where do pork chops come from?

374
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:04,600
Well, pigs. And lamb chops are?

375
00:19:05,660 --> 00:19:08,760
Baaaaaah! Ah -ha -ha!

376
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:13,340
You have absolutely no finesse, you butt head.

377
00:19:16,580 --> 00:19:19,940
Yeah. Thank you for going to so much trouble making the food and all.

378
00:19:20,760 --> 00:19:23,100
There was a time I would have really enjoyed it.

379
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:27,320
Yes. Well, Mike should have told us your diet was weird.

380
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:28,940
No, no, not weird.

381
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:34,080
Face -free. Oh, I can't blame Mike.

382
00:19:34,140 --> 00:19:35,380
I mean, he's been so busy lately.

383
00:19:35,540 --> 00:19:36,900
Oh, yeah, I'm sure he meant to tell us.

384
00:19:36,940 --> 00:19:38,160
It probably just slipped his mind.

385
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:40,400
Yes, normally he's the most responsible boy.

386
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:42,940
I'm gonna kill him.

387
00:19:44,540 --> 00:19:45,500
Oh, good night.

388
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:46,640
Yeah, good night, Kate.

389
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,480
Hi, Kate. Oh, gosh, this is a lovely variegated philodendron.

390
00:19:52,820 --> 00:19:54,540
Would you like me to heat it up for you?

391
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:02,660
Well, Kate, for a city girl, you know a lot about plants.

392
00:20:03,080 --> 00:20:04,000
Oh, I'm from Utica.

393
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:05,600
My whole family's farmers.

394
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:06,760
Hear that, Maggie?

395
00:20:07,180 --> 00:20:08,800
Farm people, salt of the earth.

396
00:20:08,980 --> 00:20:10,440
Low sodium, I'm sure.

397
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:14,780
Well, why don't we walk Kate to her car, then?

398
00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:16,820
Shall we? Well, hey, this has been great.

399
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,060
Next time, you come to my place and I'll cook for you.

400
00:20:19,480 --> 00:20:22,180
Well, the leaves will be falling off the trees soon.

401
00:20:23,260 --> 00:20:24,480
I'll bring dessert.

402
00:20:28,240 --> 00:20:30,300
Was chocolate ever alive?

403
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:35,220
No, honey. Except for those little bunnies at Easter.

404
00:20:35,220 --> 00:21:03,780
Ah! Ah! So this is how you eat?

405
00:21:04,520 --> 00:21:07,140
You just put on that apron and you steal food?

406
00:21:07,740 --> 00:21:08,980
Yeah, but I gotta get a new apron.

407
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:10,520
Corned beef here is really fatty.

408
00:21:11,940 --> 00:21:13,060
Luke, no more stealing.

409
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:14,200
No more lies, all right?

410
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:16,220
I can't let you live on a street like this.

411
00:21:16,360 --> 00:21:18,300
Oh, you can't let me?

412
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:20,520
Well, this morning I wasn't on the street.

413
00:21:20,620 --> 00:21:24,640
I had a place to sleep and a place to eat and a promising academic career.

414
00:21:24,780 --> 00:21:27,740
Now, thanks to you, I'm a homeless, hungry dropout.

415
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:29,580
Who are you, Charles Keating?

416
00:21:31,220 --> 00:21:34,140
It's just that I want a better life for you than the one you've been living.

417
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:36,000
Is that right?

418
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:37,600
Well, you got any suggestions?

419
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:44,740
Okay, all right, go on in.

420
00:21:45,780 --> 00:21:46,880
Are you sure this is okay?

421
00:21:47,040 --> 00:21:47,740
Yeah, yeah, it's fine.

422
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:48,780
You can sleep in my sister's room.

423
00:21:49,860 --> 00:21:50,700
Don't worry, it's fine.

424
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:53,840
She's away at college and you can talk to my dad in the morning, okay?

425
00:21:54,380 --> 00:21:55,140
I don't know.

426
00:21:55,300 --> 00:21:56,340
No, no, no, stop worrying.

427
00:21:56,360 --> 00:21:57,520
It's fine. Just go on upstairs.

428
00:21:57,580 --> 00:21:58,720
The first door on the right.

429
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:02,180
And sometimes she keeps this big stuffed dog on her bed.

430
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:03,200
Just shove it off and hop in.

431
00:22:05,260 --> 00:22:06,500
Okay. Good night.

432
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:15,820
Good night. Oh, no, I missed dinner.

433
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:17,800
Kate's gonna kill me.

434
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:20,520
Well, at least nothing else can go wrong tonight.

435
00:22:21,780 --> 00:22:24,780
Ah! Ah! Ah!

436
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:52,560
The end.
